  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\n[Create an element for a token](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/parsing.html#create-an-element-for-the-token) looks up the registry from the intended parent:\n\n> Let *registry* be the result of looking up a custom element registry given *intendedParent*.\n\nFor content inside a `<template for>`, [insert a foreign element](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/parsing.html#insert-a-foreign-element) passes the target parent of the [appropriate place for inserting a node](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/parsing.html#appropriate-place-for-inserting-a-node). For a template with a non-null insertion target, that's the insertion target:\n\n> If *targetParent*'s insertion end marker is not null and its parent is *patchInsertionTarget*, then return (*patchInsertionTarget*, *targetParent*'s insertion end marker).\n\nSo patched content gets the same registry as content parsed in place at the marker. Chrome Canary 157 uses the parser's registry instead:\n\n```html\n<script>customElements.define(\"x-foo\", class extends HTMLElement {});</script>\n<div id=host><template shadowrootmode=open shadowrootcustomelementregistry><x-foo id=direct></x-foo><?marker name=\"m\"><template for=m><x-foo id=patched></x-foo></template></template></div>\n```\n\nThe shadow root's custom element registry is null. Per spec, both `x-foo` elements have a null registry and stay undefined. In Chrome Canary 157, `#direct` does, but `#patched` has the global registry and is upgraded.\n\nGecko's in-progress implementation ([bug 2053472](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2053472)) also uses the parser's registry.\n\nThe spec's behavior seems more consistent, since patching shouldn't change how content parsed at the marker is created. If so, this needs a WPT.\n\ncc @foolip @noamr\n",
